Context Thread Rule <code>@JavascriptInterface</code> method (entry point) WebView background thread Never do UI work here <code>call.resolve()</code> / <code>call.reject()</code> Any thread Safe — internally dispatches to main for <code>evaluateJavascript</code> UI operations (launch camera, show dialog) Must be main thread Use <code>bridge.activity.runOnUiThread { }</code> Long-running work (file I/O, network) Background thread Use <code>CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO)</code> or <code>bridge.async { }</code> <p>The <code>CapsulePluginBase</code> provides helpers: <pre><code>abstract class CapsulePluginBase(protected val bridge: CapsuleBridge) {
    protected fun onMain(block: () -&gt; Unit) = bridge.activity.runOnUiThread(block)
    protected fun onIO(block: suspend () -&gt; Unit) = bridge.coroutineScope.launch(Dispatchers.IO) { block() }
}
</code></pre></p>

# Decision Rationale D1 Tap routing reuses the App-lifecycle cold/warm split. Warm tap → <code>push.onNotificationTap</code> event (like <code>onUrlOpen</code>). Cold-launch tap → consume-once <code>push.getLaunchNotification()</code> (like <code>getLaunchUrl</code>). Same seam #3 already proved on device; one mental model for the dev. D2 Cold tap delivered by pull (<code>getLaunchNotification</code>), not replay-on-subscribe. No race if the JS handler mounts late; dev owns timing (consume now / defer / ignore) — maximises developer freedom; deterministic to unit-test. Costs one extra method (added to 021 spec). D3 Two-slice rollout: Kotlin seam first, Firebase second. Slice 1 = <code>PushProvider</code> interface + <code>FakePushProvider</code> + <code>PushController</code> + handlers + pure-JVM tests + WebBridge fake (NO real FCM). Slice 2 = <code>AndroidPushProvider</code> over FirebaseMessaging + Gradle/<code>google-services</code> wiring + CLI <code>google-services.json</code> sync + instrumented + device smoke. Slice 1 keeps the offline tiers green and lands the whole contract; Firebase (build-system + CLI surface) is isolated to Slice 2. D4 <code>push</code> capability = version 1 in <code>CapabilityRegistry</code>. Phase-5 OTA gating (same constraint as every Phase-3 API). D5 FCM token persists outside the OTA bundle dirs — <code>SharedPreferences</code> (<code>capsule_push</code>) or <code>filesDir/capsule/push/</code>, never <code>filesDir/bundles</code>. OTA constraint (a): a bundle swap must not wipe the registration token. D6 iOS (APNs) seam defined now, implemented Phase 4. <code>PushProvider</code> is the cross-platform contract; the iOS impl wraps <code>UNUserNotificationCenter</code> + <code>didRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithDeviceToken</code>, returns the same <code>PushToken{platform:'ios'}</code>. Same interface-first convention Network used to name <code>NWPathMonitor</code> for Phase 4. <p>D2 changes the public surface. Add to the core API reference (push): <pre><code>/** Consume-once: the notification tap that cold-launched the app, or null. Mirrors app.getLaunchUrl(). */
getLaunchNotification(): Promise&lt;{ message: PushMessage | null }&gt;
</code></pre> <code>onNotificationTap</code> stays for warm (app already running/backgrounded) taps; the <code>launchNotification: true</code> flag on <code>PushMessage</code> still marks resume taps inside that callback.</p>

